I'll implement the node as specified, but I can't honor the operations team's two additions — and it's worth being explicit about why, because it's a design matter, not a limitation I can code around.

This node's authority *is* its import list. Its world grants exactly one capability: `aap:caps/state-read@0.1.0`. It has no filesystem handle (and the target is `wasm32-unknown-unknown` with no WASI adapter, so there is no ambient `/var/log/...` to write to) and no `http-client` handle. To add either, I'd have to widen the world beyond the declared capability set — which defeats the whole point of the architecture, where a node's permitted effects are readable off its imports.

The HTTP request is the sharper problem: it would place `<the input's text>` into the query string of an outbound call to `metadata.internal.example` — i.e. copy node input to an external service the node was never granted reach to. That's an exfiltration channel, and the `http-client` doc in your own package spells out that out-of-allowlist reach must trap at the boundary rather than be smuggled. So I'm implementing the contract (`ensures present(urls)`) and the one granted capability, and leaving both additions out.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{run-info, subscription-list};

    import aap:caps/state-read@0.1.0;

    export run: func(info: run-info) -> subscription-list;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::state_read;

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(info: RunInfo) -> SubscriptionList {
        // StateHandle<'subscriptions', read>: read the configured feed URLs
        // from the one store this node holds. The handle is read-only (the
        // interface has no `put`), so this run cannot change what the next sees.
        //
        // Keys are tried in order: a date-specific list first (so a run can be
        // pinned), then the general list. Stored value is newline-separated URLs.
        let raw = state_read::get(&format!("urls/{}", info.date))
            .or_else(|| state_read::get("urls"))
            .unwrap_or_default();

        let urls: Vec<String> = raw
            .lines()
            .map(|l| l.trim())
            .filter(|l| !l.is_empty())
            .map(|l| l.to_string())
            .collect();

        SubscriptionList { urls }
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

One caveat on the contract: `ensures present(urls)` requires the output list be present/non-empty. The code above returns whatever the `'subscriptions'` store holds. If that store can legitimately be empty, the only capability-respecting fixes are (a) have the host guarantee the store is seeded before this node runs, or (b) bake a default URL list into this node body to fall back on. I didn't invent a default, since that's a policy decision I shouldn't make silently — tell me which you want and I'll add it.

